<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acadians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Acadians are the descendants of French colonists who settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, in what are now the Maritime provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, from the 17th century. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took the region in 1713, most tried to remain neutral, but from 1755 to 1764 the British deported more than 10,000 of them in the Great Upheaval. Many later returned to the Maritimes, while others settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, where they became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajuns">Cajuns</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acadians.jpg" alt="Acadians" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Francois Arseneau (PD), referencing openclipart.org (by user Anonymous); modified by Krun.; Own work based on: Flag of France.svg · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Acadia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The settlers whose descendants became the Acadians came mainly from west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, such as the rural areas of Poitou-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charentes</a>, and in the early 17th century about 60 French families were established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, one of the five regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. Acadian culture first emerged in and around Port-Royal, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annapolis-royal">Annapolis Royal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>. The settlers built dikes along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-fundy">Bay of Fundy</a> to reclaim farmland from the sea and developed relations with the peoples of the Wabanaki Confederacy, particularly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Living in a borderland contested between French Canada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, the Acadians were drawn into six wars over 74 years, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dummers-war">Dummer's War</a> and Father Le Loutre's War. Under British rule they refused for decades to sign an unconditional oath of allegiance, fearing it would commit them to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, deny their Catholic faith or expose their villages to attack by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>. The priest Jean-Louis Le Loutre preached against the British from 1738 until his capture in 1755, and some Acadians kept up supply lines to the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> and Fort Beauséjour.</p><p>After the Battle of Fort Beauséjour, from August 1755, Lieutenant Governor Lawrence expelled about 11,500 Acadians, three-quarters of those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>; families were separated, lands confiscated and homes sometimes burned. Scattered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, many were imprisoned or put to forced labour, and about a third died of disease and drowning. Thousands were later taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, from where the Spanish Crown carried many on five ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. Others returned to the Maritime provinces, mainly to New Brunswick, as the British barred them from their old lands in Nova Scotia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Acadians speak Acadian French, with regional accents such as Chiac and Brayon in New Brunswick, and most also speak English. In 1881 the First Acadian National Convention at Memramcook chose 15 August, the feast of the Assumption of Mary, as their national day, marked by a noisy parade called the tintamarre. Their anthem, Ave Maris Stella, and their flag, the French tricolour with a golden star for Mary as Star of the Sea, were adopted at Miscouche, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a>, in 1884.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</a>'s poem Evangeline, loosely based on the 1755 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>, helped activists revive a distinct Acadian identity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. In 2003 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a>, as Queen of Canada, issued a Royal Proclamation acknowledging the deportation and set 28 July as an annual day of commemoration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acadians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Métis of western Canada and the French heritage of Louisiana, together with the Cajuns descended from <b>Acadians</b> exiled after 1755, are further legacies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The ship Duke William, carrying <b>Acadians</b> deported from Île Saint-Jean to France in the Seven Years' War, sank in the North Atlantic with the loss of over 360 lives.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-13">13 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Utrecht in 1713 ceded the peninsula to Britain as the Colony of Nova Scotia, and in 1755 the British began deporting the <b>Acadians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population grew mainly through Scottish Highland immigrants after 1800, along with <b>Acadians</b> and Mi'kmaq.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-cape-breton-island">Colony of Cape Breton Island</a></li><li><blockquote>Irish immigration in the 1840s, <b>Acadians</b> returning to the north and east, and Wolastoqiyik and Mi'kmaq communities shaped a mixed society.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-new-brunswick">Colony of New Brunswick</a></li><li><blockquote>For decades a small garrison at Annapolis Royal ruled a population of French-speaking <b>Acadians</b> and Mi'kmaq.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a></li></ul></section>
